WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
I taught Darsi Tiny a new

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.650
bus tool on my phone.

3
00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.810
Seven bus questions out of eight: right!

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.530
Then I asked about the weather,

5
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.790
and it called no tool at all.

6
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.130
It learned something new, and

7
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.250
forgot what it knew.

8
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.280
Today: changing a model safely, and

9
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.120
guardrails that don’t depend on it.

10
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.280
Fine-tuning changes a model’s

11
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.480
numbers from examples.

12
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.410
On the phone, only Darsi Tiny’s top layer

13
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.690
learns: sixteen examples, fifteen steps.

14
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.880
That’s up to about eleven

15
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.200
minutes on an old phone.

16
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.460
With no old conversations mixed in, it

17
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.800
gets seven bus questions out of eight,

18
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.540
but only one old skill in eight.

19
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.130
Mix in sixteen, and it keeps five.

20
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.470
Mix in forty-eight, and it keeps all

21
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.630
eight, while the bus drops to five.

22
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.270
That mixing is called replay.

23
00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.300
Quick check!

24
00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.050
Which of these fine-tuned models would

25
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.030
you put in front of real users?

26
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.980
The one with forty-eight mixed

27
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.540
in, and only after it passes the

28
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.700
whole test set again.

29
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.690
A new tool is worth little if

30
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:15.970
the old skills are gone.

31
00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.490
Every fine-tune is a new

32
00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.490
model: evaluate it like one.

33
00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.870
Darsi Tiny also learned to refuse harmful

34
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.950
requests, and to ask before sending.

35
00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.530
But requests it should refuse,

36
00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.030
reworded, still got a tool call

37
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.730
twenty-eight percent of the time in

38
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.430
English, and twelve in Arabic.

39
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.480
Yes, send it, on its own,

40
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.280
makes it invent a message.

41
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.070
And fine-tuning changes the very

42
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.590
numbers behind those habits.

43
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.740
So put the guardrails in

44
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.320
code, around the model.

45
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.370
Give each task the fewest tools.

46
00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.870
Approve the exact action, check

47
00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.430
every detail, and log everything.

48
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.600
Then re-run the evaluation

49
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.020
after every change.

50
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.000
Why the exact action?

51
00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.410
If the app only remembers your

52
00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.510
yes, the model could send a

53
00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.030
different text to someone else.

54
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.690
So compare what will run with

55
00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:15.410
what you were shown.

56
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.220
If anything differs, ask again.

57
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.460
In this lesson’s code,

58
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.060
you’ll write the gate.

59
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.800
It runs a call only if it is

60
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.020
exactly the approved one, and

61
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.440
logs the decision either way.

62
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.180
In the app, ask about a

63
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.280
bus before teaching.

64
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.540
Teach with forty-eight old

65
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.040
conversations mixed in, and test

66
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.100
the new tool and the old skills.

67
00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.880
Then teach with none, and compare.

68
00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:43.750
Your turn.

69
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.600
A model has learned to ask

70
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.460
before it sends a message.

71
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.690
Why still check the approval in code?

72
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.180
Because learned behaviour can fail.

73
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.320
Remember: fine-tuning teaches and forgets,

74
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.420
so mix old examples in and test both.

75
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.460
Learned safety is not a guarantee.

76
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.150
Guard actions in code: fewest tools, exact

77
00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.290
approval, checks, logs and tests.

78
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.340
Try it yourself: write a guardrail plan

79
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.060
for an agent you’d like to build.

80
00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.110
List its tools, the actions needing

81
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.750
approval, the checks in code, the

82
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.010
logs, and the tests you’ll re-run.
